## Hard rules (binding — `AWS-prescriptive` for Catalyst)

### B-1. Pin base images by digest, never by `:latest`

`FROM repo/image@sha256:<digest>` — not `FROM repo/image:tag` and never
`FROM repo/image:latest`. Tags drift; digests are stable. The Trivy /
Scout SBOM jobs call out drift in PR comments.

```dockerfile
# Good
FROM public.ecr.aws/docker/library/python:3.12-slim@sha256:<verified-digest>

# Bad
FROM python:3.12
FROM python:latest
```

### B-2. Minimal / distroless / Amazon Linux 2023 minimal only

The base layer MUST be one of:

- A **distroless** image (`gcr.io/distroless/<runtime>`) — no shell, no
  package manager, smallest attack surface.
- An **Amazon Linux 2023 minimal** image (`public.ecr.aws/amazonlinux/amazonlinux:2023-minimal`) —
  AWS-supported, fast security patching cadence, fits the AWS-prescriptive
  bias.
- A `*-slim` or `*-alpine` variant of an upstream language base image (e.g.,
  `python:3.12-slim`, `node:20-alpine`) when distroless is not viable.

Standard Debian / Ubuntu / Fedora base images are a hard reject — too
much surface, too many CVEs, longer scan-time.

### B-3. Multi-stage builds — strip the build toolchain

Build dependencies (compilers, package managers, dev headers) NEVER appear
in the runtime layer. Use multi-stage builds:

```dockerfile
# build stage — has the toolchain
FROM golang:1.22@sha256:<digest> AS build
WORKDIR /src
COPY . .
RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags="-s -w" -o /out/app ./cmd/app

# runtime stage — distroless, just the binary
FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot@sha256:<digest>
COPY --from=build /out/app /app
USER nonroot:nonroot
ENTRYPOINT ["/app"]
```

### B-4. Non-root user

Runtime stage MUST set `USER` to a non-root UID. Distroless `:nonroot` tags
provide this; for non-distroless bases, create a user explicitly:

```dockerfile
RUN adduser -D -u 10001 app
USER app:app
```

### B-5. `HEALTHCHECK` instruction

Every image declares a `HEALTHCHECK` so the runtime (ECS / EKS / Lambda
container image) can act on it. ECS task definitions surface this directly
to the ALB target health check.

```dockerfile
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=10s --retries=3 \
  CMD ["/app", "healthcheck"]
```

### B-6. No secrets at build time

`COPY` of `.env`, credential files, or secret literals into the image is a
hard reject. Secrets resolve at *runtime* via Secrets Manager / SSM
references on the task definition, not at build time.

### B-7. Documented `.trivyignore` exceptions only

CVEs we knowingly accept go in `.trivyignore` at repo root, with a rationale
comment for each entry:

```
# rationale: upstream not yet fixed; runtime mitigation = X; review-by 2026-Q3
CVE-2025-12345
# rationale: not exploitable in our context (Y subsystem unused); review-by 2026-Q4
CVE-2025-67890
```

The persona reviews `.trivyignore` quarterly and opens a `type/kaizen` for
any entry past its `review-by` date.

### B-8. Image labels for traceability

Every image MUST set OCI image labels for chargeback / attribution:

```dockerfile
ARG GIT_SHA
ARG BUILT_AT
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="{{service_or_module_name}}"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.source="https://github.com/Cloud-Byte-Consulting/Catalyst"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.revision="${GIT_SHA}"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.created="${BUILT_AT}"
LABEL com.catalyst.tenant="{{tenant}}"
LABEL com.catalyst.application="{{application}}"
```

## Enforcement

- **CI**: the `generate-container-scan-workflow` (Trivy) or
  `generate-container-scan-scout-workflow` job fails the build on any of
  B-1..B-7 violations the scanner can detect (B-1 via Trivy's misconfig
  check; B-3..B-6 via Hadolint sidecar in the same workflow when enabled).
- **Registry**: ECR scan-on-push (basic) + Inspector V2 (enhanced) per the
  `generate-ecr-scan-on-push` capability. Findings open
  `type/ops-intel-finding` issues via the existing webhook-handler.
- **Review**: every Dockerfile PR triggers the `aws-security-engineer`
  prompt review.

